# 41. What "Any App" Means

Deadpool should not maintain a custom integration for every application.

Instead:

```text
Application
     ↓
Windows UI Automation
     ↓
UI elements
     ↓
Mouse/keyboard fallback
     ↓
Screenshot/vision fallback
```

This allows the same control architecture to operate:

```text
Chrome
Edge
Word
Excel
PowerPoint
VS Code
Antigravity
Cursor
Notepad
File Explorer
Discord
Spotify
IDEs
custom applications
```

Applications with accessible UI will work best.

Applications with unusual/custom-rendered interfaces may require screenshot/vision or application-specific adapters.

---

# 42. Important Engineering Reality

"Any app" cannot honestly mean:

> "Every application will always work perfectly without any adaptation."

Some applications:

* expose poor accessibility information
* render custom graphics
* require authentication
* use anti-automation mechanisms
* depend on hardware
* expose no semantic UI
* change their UI frequently

Therefore Deadpool must have a fallback hierarchy:

```text
Semantic UI
    ↓
Application API
    ↓
Keyboard
    ↓
Mouse
    ↓
Screenshot/OCR
    ↓
Vision
    ↓
Ask user
```

This provides broad generality without pretending that every application is identical.

---

# 43. Security Principle

Deadpool must follow:

> **Observe broadly, act narrowly.**

It can inspect the environment to understand the task, but actions that create meaningful external or destructive effects require stronger authorization.

Never give the LLM unrestricted:

```text
cmd.exe
PowerShell
Python
registry modification
```

as raw execution tools.

Wrap them behind controlled interfaces.

